%I A101284 #16 Oct 06 2026 09:07:09
%S A101284 5,9,11,17,29,35,39,51,65,71,77,95,101,107,117,129,131,137,141,149,
%T A101284 161,179,185,201,215,239,249,267,269,287,297,299,305,309,315,327,329,
%U A101284 339,341,347,371,375,381,401,407,429,431,441,449,459,471,479
%N A101284 Numbers k such that a type-8 Gaussian normal basis exists for GF(2^k) over GF(2).
%C A101284 A type-t Gaussian normal basis exists for GF(2^k) if and only if p = k*t+1 is prime and gcd(k, (p-1)/ord(2 mod p)) = 1.
%C A101284 From _Jianing Song_, Oct 06 2026: (Start)
%C A101284 Note that p = k*t+1 being prime and gcd(k, (p-1)/ord(2 mod p)) = 1 imply that (p-1)/ord(2 mod p) divides t. In particular, k is a term in this sequence if and only if k satisfies one the following conditions:
%C A101284 (i) ord(2 mod p) = p-1 (impossible since 2 is a quadratic residue modulo p = 8*k + 1);
%C A101284 (ii) k odd, and ord(2 mod p) = (p-1)/2 (k = 5, 17, 39, 51, 65, ...);
%C A101284 (iii) k odd, and ord(2 mod p) = (p-1)/4 (k = 35, 77, 129, 131, 137, ...);
%C A101284 (iv) k odd, and ord(2 mod p) = (p-1)/8 (k = 9, 11, 29, 117, 161, ...). (End)
%H A101284 Jianing Song, <a href="/A101284/b101284.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A101284 Joerg Arndt, <a href="https://www.jjj.de/fxt/#fxtbook">Matters Computational (The Fxtbook)</a>, section 42.9  "Gaussian normal bases", pp.914-920.
%o A101284 (PARI) isok(n) = (isprime(p=8*n+1) && gcd(n, (p-1)/znorder(Mod(2, p))) == 1); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Aug 18 2013
%Y A101284 Cf. A071642 (type-1), A054639 (type-2), A136415 (type-3), A137310 (type-4), A137311 (type-5), A137313 (type-6), A137314 (type-7).
